{
  "dom_id": "step-q50349",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6a64064f114ac2e6acafa791c4fda50849cad6f3387c4793bdf991c95ae8e542.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{x}{y+1}$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=x+y$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{x}{y-1}$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6a119b24c773a25bf3530dbfb8574c710cc37a5e970d92112aee5f489551ef02.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0.$\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{x}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0,$ and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{\u2032}|=|\\frac{x}{y+1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to -1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=x+y$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0$ (the value depends on $y$).\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{x}{y-1}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0,$ but for $x\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=|\\frac{x}{y-1}|\\to \\frac{|x|}{2}\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
